WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.694
Scenario-specific playbooks apply the same structure to different incident types. The structure stays familiar: purpose, scope, triggers, severity, roles, data sources, response actions, decision points, evidence handling, communications, containment, recovery, closure, and post-incident tasks.

2
00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:19.712
The content changes because each scenario has different signals, risks, and responders.

3
00:00:19.832 --> 00:00:25.138
A phishing playbook may start from a user report or email security alert.

4
00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:36.687
It should guide responders to preserve message metadata, identify recipients, check whether links were clicked or credentials were submitted, quarantine related messages when appropriate, and coordinate user communication.

5
00:00:36.807 --> 00:00:43.745
A ransomware playbook may focus on affected hosts, business continuity, backups, containment, executive escalation, and recovery planning.

6
00:00:43.865 --> 00:00:49.988
A malware infection playbook may focus on endpoint telemetry, isolation decisions, artifact removal, and verification.

7
00:00:50.108 --> 00:00:59.904
A lost or stolen device playbook may involve device management, encryption status, remote lock or wipe options, user follow-up, and data exposure assessment.

8
00:01:00.024 --> 00:01:11.044
Identity-focused scenarios need careful access review. A suspicious login playbook may use authentication logs, device information, multi-factor events, impossible travel patterns, and recent account activity.

9
00:01:11.164 --> 00:01:19.736
A privileged account compromise playbook should add stronger escalation, token revocation, password reset, role review, audit log review, and approval requirements.

10
00:01:19.856 --> 00:01:35.774
A cloud key exposure playbook should identify where the key was found, what identity it belongs to, what permissions it had, what audit logs show, whether resources were created or changed, and how rotation or revocation will affect systems.

11
00:01:35.894 --> 00:01:48.139
Data exfiltration and web application compromise playbooks usually require broad coordination. They may involve application logs, network telemetry, storage logs, legal and privacy review, customer impact analysis, and careful communications.

12
00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:58.871
The playbook should stay defensive and evidence-focused. It should help responders understand scope, protect systems, preserve records, and coordinate remediation without turning into offensive guidance.

13
00:01:58.991 --> 00:02:05.930
Environment-specific details make playbooks useful, but too much hard-coded detail makes them hard to maintain.

14
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:13.805
Use stable references where possible: group names, evidence locations, ticket templates, detection names, dashboard links, and role-based contacts.

15
00:02:13.925 --> 00:02:22.904
Avoid embedding personal contact details or tool steps that change every month unless the organization has a process to keep them current.

16
00:02:23.024 --> 00:02:30.780
If a technical action is complex, link to a maintained runbook rather than copying stale instructions into every playbook.

17
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:39.471
Playbooks should connect to operations. Link them to detection logic, response tools, ticket templates, evidence repositories, communication templates, and tabletop exercises.

18
00:02:39.591 --> 00:02:44.897
Test them with realistic scenarios. After real incidents, update them with lessons learned.

19
00:02:45.017 --> 00:02:55.629
A playbook that is never tested is only a guess. A playbook that is owned, exercised, and improved becomes part of the team's response muscle memory.
